when it comes to cinematic Terror in the  water we've got sharks in the Pacific  we've got sharks in the Atlantic and  we've even got sharks in the streets of  Los Angeles here are the shark movies  you have to see before you die  bait 3D is the 2012 Australian  Singaporean movie about people who  survive a freak tidal wave only to be  trapped in a partially submerged grocery  store and hunted by a pack of  bloodthirsty great whites and it sounds  like a special breed of made for TV  stupidity but it's actually a  surprisingly tense ride the stranded  crew must deal with robberies Sparks  from split wires being trapped on store  shelves without an obvious way out and  of course plenty of close calls with  carnivorous monster fish Bates initial  critical reception was lukewarm it  currently holds a 47 rating on Rotten  Tomatoes but if you're the type of  moviegoer who Thrills at Creature  Feature genre fair you should find  plenty to love here a sequel called Deep  Water about a plane crashing in the  Pacific Ocean was planned for a 2014  release but it was yanked from the  schedule after it was decided that the  plot was too similar to the Malaysian  Airlines flight 370 disaster still the  first one holds up well enough for a  night in with a full bowl of popcorn  technically the Meg isn't really about a  shark at all but it's close enough that  we absolutely had to include it based on  Steve Alton's 1997 book Meg a novel of  deep Terror this goofy 2018 sci-fi  thriller Stars Jason Statham is the  leader of a rescue mission that heads to  the floor of the Pacific Ocean that's  where the characters run smack into a  monstrous 75-foot Megalodon a  prehistoric Titan that makes great  whites look like goldfish it was thought  to be extinct but it turns out it is  very much alive and very hungry to give  you an idea of how seriously to take  this movie there's a scene in which the  characters watch the Beast devour a  humpback whale and a single Chomp right  outside their underwater research  station and there's another moment when  they immobilize it by ramming into its  midsection with a submarine a swarm of  smaller more modern sharks that are  attracted to its blood then set upon it  like piranhas and eat it alive the Meg  is certainly no Masterpiece but it's  more than enough fun to justify a watch  as Forbes critic Scott Mendelson put it  the Meg is fun plain and simple it's a  polished B movie that delivers the goods  if not the greats and it's worth  whatever your local theater charges for  a weekend matinee is there anything more  we can ask of a big budget Creature  Feature Chomp away be safe yeah thanks  Bill  the 2017 release 47 meters down explores  the ultimate fear of anyone who's ever  watched a video of a cage dive in shark  infested waters what if the cable snaps  well there's that and then there's also  the fear of the Sharks nudging their  weight Between the Bars in this movie  the cable doesn't eat snap and sisters  Lisa and Kate quickly find themselves  trapped with a dwindling oxygen supply  on an ocean floor patrolled by hungry  great whites 47 meters down offers a  great little twist on the typical shark  movie formula that doesn't push things  too far it didn't blow every critic away  but the fact that this little sharp  flick had as much bite as it did was the  surprise in and of itself varieties Joe  Laden enthused director Johannes Roberts  mostly underwater Thriller is a compact  and sturdily crafted Bee Movie that  generates enough scares and suspense to  qualify as well maybe not a pleasant  surprise but a reasonably entertaining  one there's no reason that Sharknado  should actually work its title isn't a  particularly clever play on words this  is literally a movie about a tornado  filled with sharks that throws the Beast  all over Greater Los Angeles and a group  of survivors who leap into their mouths  with chainsaws but surely you knew that  already and you probably didn't know how  to feel about this movie when you first  saw it on the surface it looks like any  other schlocky made for TV sci-fi movie  but this one refuses to make the same  mistakes others in its goofy genre do by  taking itself even slightly seriously it  knows it's absurd you can laugh at it  but it's right there laughing at itself  right alongside you Sharknado went on to  win over the masses and even some  critics as well bizarrely it somehow  currently holds a 78 score on Rotten  Tomatoes without a doubt it was one of  the most talked about films of 2013.  once again and we cannot stress this  enough this is a movie about a tornado  filled with sharks the New York daily  news's David Hinckley said it best when  he declared Sharknado is an hour and a  half of your life that you'll never get  back and you won't want to but even a  movie that subverts expectations this  hard ultimately couldn't save itself  from five lame sequels each subject to  increasingly diminishing returns still  the 2013 original is an utterly bizarre  gloriously brainless wonderful little  gem  we'll just come right out and say it  Alzheimer's experiments involving  genetically modified animals rarely end  well for movie characters but we didn't  have to wait for 2011's rise at the  Planet of the Apes to figure that out we  only needed to have seen deep blue sea a  silly but thoroughly fun 1999 sci-fi  horror flick in which genetically  engineered super smart sharks rampaged  through an underwater lab the enhanced  shark brains that make these monsters so  lethal and by just as many comparisons  to Jurassic Park's Velociraptors as they  do to the big bad from Jaws good luck  getting trapped in a Sea Lab with these  things lurking around  deep blue sea was reasonably well  received when it came out but its Legacy  has grown even more with time writing  for Wired in 2016 Brian Rafferty offered  High Praise calling it quotes the  greatest non-jaws shark movie of all  time this was thanks to its inventive  action set pieces and fairly  well-rounded human characters other  moments like Samuel L Jackson's  Unforgettable death scene and the Very  idea of intelligent sharks have led  plenty of other viewers to declare deep  blue sea one of the best shark flicks  ever made after all it's hard to argue  with the combination of Sam Jackson and  hungry animals  the jaw series may be the earliest  available proof that capturing lightning  in a bottle once doesn't mean that  sequels will be able to do the same the  franchise ultimately got crushed under  the weight of diminishing returns with  the release of joyless flops like jaws  3D and Jaws the Revenge but 1978's Jaws  2 in which the first film's Chief Brody  faces off against another great white  and a battle to save the lives of his  stranded children and their friends  isn't half bad it doesn't quite  recapture the magic of the original but  it wasn't reasonable to expect it to in  the first place Jaws 2 is 10 swell acted  well-paced occasionally funny and  jam-packed with all the killer shark  action fans of the original had come to  expect there's a scene in which the  shark leaps out of the water and  literally brings down the helicopter  sending propeller shards through the  sails of the stranded boats and there's  another in which we get to revisit Chief  Brody's habit of killing sharks by  jamming explosives into their mouths  what more could you want  foreign  Australia gets another entry on this  list in the form of 2010's the reef  after all what other country could you  trust to be an authority on the  existential danger of nature this movie  follows a group of friends whose  sailboat capsizes on their way to  Indonesia naturally a great white shark  catches wind of this and decides to swim  in to try to grab some lunch one of the  most notable aspects of this movie is  that it features actual footage of  sharks as opposed to lifeless  animatronics or cringe-worthy CGI  overall The Reef works because it knows  exactly what it is it also knows what  the limitations of its genre are and it  doesn't try to entertain Beyond its  means it has a tight script with no fat  to trim and there's absolutely nothing  wrong with that a French review site put  it perfectly when it declared the reef  turns out to be a good little friendly  film that was able to demonstrate a good  dose of realism and efficiency  unlike a lot of other movies on this  list Open Water doesn't Revel in glepel  over the top absurdity instead it's a  gritty tense terrifying Thriller that's  Loosely based on the true story of Tom  and Eileen Lonergan married scuba divers  who were accidentally left behind by  their dive group near the Great Barrier  Reef and never seen again since little  is known about what actually happened to  the actual loner against the makers of  Open Water had to use their imaginations  to tell its story of course sharks are  involved and that may very well be the  most obvious Peril in a story about  people stranded at sea but it's not the  only life-threatening problem scenes of  the couple starving struggling with  extreme thirst and exposure and being  tortured by mental anguish as they wait  for the inevitable all add to a sense of  inescapable dread by the time the Sharks  swing by it's practically merciful  critics Heap to claim on the film few of  them praising it as highly as Roger  Ebert who raved rarely but sometimes a  movie can have an actual physical effect  on you it gets under your defenses and  sidesteps the it's only a movie reflex  and creates a visceral feeling that  might as well be real so check out Open  Water if you dare  it's the stuff of nightmares imagine  being completely alone stranded on the  bloating carcass of a half-eaten whale  while a great white shark prowls around  with a whip of your blood and an empty  stomach that's the situation that Blake  Lively finds herself in in the shallows  an airtight lethally crafted Thriller  that had critics cheering mad solar  sites wrote on Roger ebert.com Lively is  superb here giving one of those  hyper-focused action-led performances  that's as much an athletic feat as an  aesthetic one and IGN's Simon Thompson  raved the shallows does for surfing with  the Blair Witch Project did for camping  and makes Jaws look like a children's  tea party played out with precision and  skill it delivers everything it suggests  it will and then some terrifyingly good  it should come as no surprise that  1975's Jaws is the Crown Jewel on this  list what other choice could there have  ever possibly been Steven Spielberg's  horror Thriller Masterpiece practically  invented the modern Blockbuster and  unlike a lot of decades-old Classics it  still holds up today for one thing the  script is well paced and endlessly  quotable here's to swimming with  bow-legged women  the characters from Roy scheiders  overwhelmed but determine Chief Roadie  to Murray Hamilton's sleazeback mayor  are believable and memorable and Quinn's  speech about the very real shark attack  on the crew of the torpedoed USS  Indianapolis in World War II is one of  the most unforgettable scenes in movie  history the scares still stand the test  of time and the Practical effects of age  better than those in almost all of the  Bargain Bin creature features that tried  and failed to recapture the magic of  this classic the baited the  skinny-dipping girl in the beginning of  the movie deserves a place among the  most chilling scares in horror movie  history  and we don't even have to mention that  simple Unforgettable dread-inducing  theme courtesy of the incomparable John  Williams we know you're already humming  it to yourself check out one of our  newest videos right here plus even more  Looper videos about your favorite stuff  are coming soon subscribe to our YouTube  channel and hit the Bell so you don't  miss a single one  [Music]

